# AshHooks guided tour

<!-- livebook:{"app_settings":{"access_type":"public","auto_run":true,"show_source":true,"locale":"en"}} -->

## You will learn

* Verify webhooks from any sender by configuration: an HMAC header, a timestamped
  structured header with a replay window, an Ed25519 signature, a signed JWS body,
  and a sender that canonicalizes form fields
* Ingest through the ledger: deduplicate a replay, reject a tamper, refuse a stale
  timestamp, and rotate a secret without dropping deliveries
* Classify events with your own table and run an idempotent handler
* Verify directly, without a ledger, and see what an invalid configuration reports
* Send an outbound webhook to an existing WebHook Tester inbox and verify its captured bytes
* Scope a multi-tenant deployment: one tenant's dispatch never reaches another's rows
* Fit the extensions to a domain that reserves `payload`, owns an `active` switch, and types its registers with atoms
* Observe everything through telemetry
* Prune old rows with the retention hooks

This notebook uses a real SQLite database and an existing WebHook Tester
receiver. The receiver must already answer at `http://127.0.0.1:52871`;
set `LOCAL_WEBHOOK_TESTER_PORT` to use another existing receiver. See
the [local receiver guide](https://github.com/baselabs/ash_hooks/blob/main/ops/local/README.md).
The notebook starts no server.
Uses ash_hooks **3.1.0**. The tour plays both sides of each webhook: it signs
requests the way a sender would and verifies them the way your application does.
Real senders, a production queue, and your HTTP endpoint come from your own
application.

<!-- livebook:{"break_marks":true} -->

## Setup

```elixir
package =
  case System.get_env("ASH_HOOKS_NOTEBOOK_SOURCE") do
    nil -> {:ash_hooks, "== 3.1.0"}
    source -> {:ash_hooks, path: Path.expand(source)}
  end

# Release verification supplies the unpacked candidate archive explicitly.
# Normal notebook execution resolves the exact published version above.
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ash, ">= 3.37.2 and < 4.0.0-0"},
    package,
    {:ash_sqlite, "~> 0.2"},
    {:jason, "~> 1.2"}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

```elixir
# Ash 3.33+ requires every application to choose how string length is
# counted before any resource compiles — this notebook is its own app.
# :codepoints is Ash's recommended value: max_length bounds value size
# and matches how SQL data layers count.
Application.put_env(:ash, :default_string_length_count, :codepoints)
"3.1.0" = Application.spec(:ash_hooks, :vsn) |> to_string()

# Keys for the tour's senders. In your application these come from your
# secret store through the inbound `secret` sources, never from literals.
#
# Shared secrets for the HMAC senders:
Application.put_env(:ash_hooks, :tour_orders_secret, :crypto.strong_rand_bytes(32))
Application.put_env(:ash_hooks, :tour_orders_previous_secret, :crypto.strong_rand_bytes(32))
Application.put_env(:ash_hooks, :tour_payments_secret, :crypto.strong_rand_bytes(32))
Application.put_env(:ash_hooks, :tour_sms_secret, :crypto.strong_rand_bytes(20))

# An Ed25519 key pair: the sender keeps the private key, you configure the
# public key (here as base64 of its 32 raw bytes).
{partner_public, partner_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
Application.put_env(:ash_hooks, :tour_partner_public_key, Base.encode64(partner_public))

# An RSA key pair for a sender that signs a JWS body. You configure its
# public key as PEM.
envelope_private = :public_key.generate_key({:rsa, 2048, 65_537})
{:RSAPrivateKey, _, modulus, exponent, _, _, _, _, _, _, _} = envelope_private
envelope_public_pem =
  :public_key.pem_encode([
    :public_key.pem_entry_encode(:SubjectPublicKeyInfo, {:RSAPublicKey, modulus, exponent})
  ])

Application.put_env(:ash_hooks, :tour_envelope_public_key, envelope_public_pem)
IO.puts(envelope_public_pem)
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Repo do
  use AshSqlite.Repo, otp_app: :ash_hooks
end
```

## Describe your senders

Each sender gets one provider module. `use AshHooks.Provider, verify: [...]`
describes how the sender signs its requests; ash_hooks checks the options when
the module compiles and generates the verification callbacks. The options come
from the sender's documentation: the header, the algorithm, how the signature
and secret are encoded, what is signed, and where the timestamp is. The
[Verify any sender](https://github.com/baselabs/ash_hooks/blob/main/documentation/tutorials/verify-any-sender.md)
guide has recipes for common shapes, and `AshHooks.Provider.Scheme` documents
every option.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.VerifiedEvent do
  defstruct [:type, :payload]
end

# 1. An HMAC-SHA256 of the raw body, lowercase hex, in one header.
#    This provider also classifies events and runs a handler.
defmodule Tour.Orders do
  use AshHooks.Provider,
    verify: [
      algorithm: :hmac_sha256,
      signature: {:header, "x-tour-signature"},
      encoding: :hex
    ]

  # Map the sender's type strings to your event atoms with an explicit
  # table, never String.to_atom on request data (that would let a hostile
  # sender grow your atom table). Unknown types fail closed.
  @types %{"order.paid" => :order_paid}

  @impl true
  def parse_event_type(%{"type" => type}) do
    case Map.fetch(@types, type) do
      {:ok, atom} -> {:ok, atom}
      :error -> {:error, :unknown_event_type}
    end
  end

  def parse_event_type(_payload), do: {:error, :malformed_payload}

  # Your application's action. It must be safe to run twice for the same
  # event: the ledger deduplicates deliveries, but a crash between this
  # call and the ledger update re-runs it on redelivery.
  @impl true
  def handle_event(type, payload) do
    Agent.update(Tour.Handled, &[payload["id"] | &1])
    {:ok, %Tour.VerifiedEvent{type: type, payload: payload}}
  end
end

# 2. A structured header carrying a timestamp and one or more signatures:
#    x-tour-payments: t=1731312000,v1=<hex>,v1=<hex>
#    The signed input is "timestamp.body"; any listed v1 that verifies is
#    accepted, so the sender can rotate keys.
defmodule Tour.Payments do
  use AshHooks.Provider,
    verify: [
      algorithm: :hmac_sha256,
      signature: {:structured_header, "x-tour-payments", signature_key: "v1"},
      timestamp: {:signature_field, "t"},
      signed_input: [:timestamp, ".", :body],
      tolerance_seconds: 300
    ]
end

# 3. A public-key signature: Ed25519 over the raw body, base64 in a header.
#    The secret source returns the sender's public key.
defmodule Tour.Partner do
  use AshHooks.Provider,
    verify: [
      algorithm: :ed25519,
      signature: {:header, "x-partner-signature"},
      encoding: :base64,
      key_encoding: :base64
    ]
end

# 4. The whole body is a compact JWS (an RS256 JWT). The algorithm is pinned
#    here and never read from the token; the verified payload is what the
#    ledger stores.
defmodule Tour.Envelope do
  use AshHooks.Provider,
    verify: [
      algorithm: :rsa_pkcs1_sha256,
      signature: :jws_body
    ]
end

# 5. A sender that canonicalizes before signing: HMAC-SHA1 over the public
#    URL followed by every form field's name and value, sorted by name. A host
#    function builds that string; returning anything but a binary fails.
defmodule Tour.Sms do
  use AshHooks.Provider,
    verify: [
      algorithm: :hmac_sha1,
      signature: {:header, "x-sms-signature"},
      encoding: :base64,
      signed_input: {__MODULE__, :signed_input}
    ]

  def signed_input(raw_body, %{request_uri: url}) when is_binary(url) do
    fields =
      raw_body
      |> URI.decode_query()
      |> Enum.sort()
      |> Enum.map(fn {name, value} -> name <> value end)

    url <> Enum.join(fields)
  end

  def signed_input(_raw_body, _ctx), do: nil
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Ledger do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tour.Domain,
    data_layer: AshSqlite.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshHooks, AshHooks.InboundDelivery]

  sqlite do
    table("tour_ledgers")
    repo(Tour.Repo)
  end

  inbound_delivery do
    scope_identity([:account_id])
  end

  attributes do
    attribute(:account_id, :string, allow_nil?: false)
    timestamps()
  end

  actions do
    defaults([:read])
  end

  # One declaration per source. `provider` is required; the name identifies
  # the source in the ledger. Secrets are sources, never literals.
  webhooks do
    inbound :orders do
      provider(Tour.Orders)
      secret fn -> {:ok, Application.fetch_env!(:ash_hooks, :tour_orders_secret)} end
      # the outgoing key during a rotation: both keys verify until you remove it
      previous_secret fn ->
        {:ok, Application.fetch_env!(:ash_hooks, :tour_orders_previous_secret)}
      end

      # the sender's event id is the deduplication identity
      event_id(&__MODULE__.event_id/1)
    end

    inbound :payments do
      provider(Tour.Payments)
      secret fn -> {:ok, Application.fetch_env!(:ash_hooks, :tour_payments_secret)} end
      # overrides the provider's tolerance_seconds for this source
      replay_window_seconds(120)
    end

    inbound :partner do
      provider(Tour.Partner)
      secret fn -> {:ok, Application.fetch_env!(:ash_hooks, :tour_partner_public_key)} end
    end

    inbound :envelope do
      provider(Tour.Envelope)
      secret fn -> {:ok, Application.fetch_env!(:ash_hooks, :tour_envelope_public_key)} end
    end

    inbound :sms do
      provider(Tour.Sms)
      body_format(:form)
      secret fn -> {:ok, Application.fetch_env!(:ash_hooks, :tour_sms_secret)} end
    end
  end

  def event_id(%{"id" => id}) when is_binary(id), do: {:ok, id}
  def event_id(_payload), do: :error
end

defmodule Tour.Endpoint do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tour.Domain,
    data_layer: AshSqlite.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshHooks.Endpoint]

  sqlite do
    table("tour_endpoints")
    repo(Tour.Repo)
  end

  actions do
    defaults([:read, :create, :update])
    default_accept(:*)
  end

  endpoint do
    # per-endpoint response capture: the four columns exist only when
    # declared (a migration is required to adopt them)
    response_capture_attributes(:on)
  end
end

defmodule Tour.Subscription do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tour.Domain,
    data_layer: AshSqlite.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshHooks.Subscription]

  sqlite do
    table("tour_subscriptions")
    repo(Tour.Repo)
  end

  actions do
    defaults([:read, :create])
    default_accept(:*)
  end

  subscription do
    endpoint_resource(Tour.Endpoint)
  end
end

defmodule Tour.Delivery do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tour.Domain,
    data_layer: AshSqlite.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshHooks.OutboundDelivery]

  sqlite do
    table("tour_deliveries")
    repo(Tour.Repo)
  end

  attributes do
    timestamps()
  end

  actions do
    defaults([:read])
  end

  outbound_delivery do
    # resend/replay bookkeeping (migrate the four columns when you adopt)
    resend_tracking(:on)
  end
end

defmodule Tour.Attempt do
  # The attempt history: one durable row per delivery attempt (time,
  # outcome, classified reason, HTTP status, latency, the signing key
  # REFERENCE). Written by the delivery runtime when the worker config
  # carries `attempts:` — the audit trail behind delivery logs and health.
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tour.Domain,
    data_layer: AshSqlite.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshHooks.OutboundAttempt]

  sqlite do
    table("tour_attempts")
    repo(Tour.Repo)
  end

  actions do
    defaults([:read])
  end
end

defmodule Tour.Order do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tour.Domain,
    data_layer: AshSqlite.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshHooks]

  sqlite do
    table("tour_orders")
    repo(Tour.Repo)
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key(:id)
  end

  actions do
    defaults([:read, :create])
  end

  webhooks do
    outbound :order_paid do
      subscriptions(Tour.Subscription)
      deliveries(Tour.Delivery)
    end
  end
end

defmodule Tour.Domain do
  use Ash.Domain, otp_app: nil, validate_config_inclusion?: false

  resources do
    resource(Tour.Ledger)
    resource(Tour.Endpoint)
    resource(Tour.Subscription)
    resource(Tour.Delivery)
    resource(Tour.Attempt)
    resource(Tour.Order)
    resource(Tour.TenantEndpoint)
    resource(Tour.TenantSubscription)
    resource(Tour.TenantDelivery)
    resource(Tour.TenantOrder)
  end
end
```

